# DURABLE REPRO — every WRITE face through a `ReadGuard[T]` field place is
# SILENTLY DISCARDED instead of rejected (Core #10, lower-or-reject).
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, all three faces `gg check`-CLEAN and
# all three building cleanly, on a guard LOCAL — no parameter indirection):
#
#   | face                | written        | prints today | intended  |
#   |---------------------|----------------|--------------|-----------|
#   | `&`-arg write-through | `inc(&rg.fd)` | 10           | REJECT    |
#   | plain assign          | `rg.fd = 99`  | 10           | REJECT    |
#   | compound assign       | `rg.fd += 5`  | 10           | REJECT    |
#
#   CONTROL (measured, same program shape): the `WriteGuard` twin
#   `inc(&wg.fd)` prints **11** — correct. So the guard machinery works; it is
#   the READ-ONLY guard that swallows writes.
#
# ⚠ SYMMETRIC ACROSS THE FACES, so this is NOT the served-at-one-face asymmetry
# of the `Guard[T]`-through-a-parameter family. It is a plain lower-or-reject
# violation: the write passes the type checker and is dropped at lowering.
#
# THE INTENDED BEHAVIOUR IS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN IN THE PRODUCER.
# `try_resolve_field_place`'s guard branch (`src/ir/lowering/exprs/mod.rs`,
# the `guard_inner_suffix` arm) early-outs with `return None` for a read-only
# guard, carrying the comment "ReadGuard: writes are forbidden — don't resolve a
# write place (type checker should reject in future)". The reference-grade fix
# is that check-time rejection, NOT a lowering change: a `ReadGuard[T]` place is
# read-only by construction, so writing through one is a type error.
#
# SCOPE — this fixture asserts the FIRST face only (`inc(&rg.fd)`); the assign
# and compound faces are recorded in the table above and are pinned by the same
# TODO entry. Root shape sampled: a guard LOCAL obtained from `RWLock.read()`.
# Not sampled: a `ReadGuard` reached through a parameter, a resource-typed
# guarded field, or the LLVM lane.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): `gg check` REJECTS the write with a diagnostic
# naming the read-only guard. This fixture is `#[ignore]`d and pins the reject;
# it asserts the error CODE loosely (`error[E_`) because the code for
# "write through a read-only place" is not yet minted — tighten it to the exact
# code the closing round chooses.
#
# The seed is COMPUTED, not a literal, so no const-fold can elide the write.
#
# Cited from TODO.md ("`ReadGuard[T]` field writes are silently DISCARDED").
from std.sync import RWLock

struct Config:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

void inc(int &n):
    n = n + 1

void main():
    RWLock[Config] lock = RWLock[Config](Config(seed()))
    ReadGuard[Config] rg = lock.read()
    inc(&rg.fd)
    print(rg.fd)
